// New results: small theorems reached while assessing proposed conjectures. Sketches,
// not refereed; each states what remains open.
export const NEW_RESULTS = [
  { key: "R1", title: "Exponential cost of conditionally independent witnesses under a conservation law", cat: "foundations", extends: ["F5", "F8"],
    status: "Proof sketch (8 November 2026), not refereed. Conjectured in an unpublished note prepared with ChatGPT Astra; the argument below was found by Claude Fable 5.1 while assessing it, and checked by the maintainer. Not located in the literature.",
    setting: r`A qubit $S$ with conserved charge $Q_S=\sigma_z/2$ is prepared in $\lvert\pm x\rangle$ (eigenstates of $\sigma_x$, equiprobable). An apparatus $A$ carries all charged auxiliaries, with $\operatorname{spec}(Q_A)\subset\{0,\dots,W\}$ and arbitrary preparation $\omega_A$. Neutral registers $Y_1,\dots,Y_R$ are written by a unitary $U$ with $[U,Q_S+Q_A]=0$ and read in a fixed basis. Target: $P^\star_{R,p}(\mathbf y\mid x)=\prod_i[(1-p)\mathbf 1_{y_i=x}+p\,\mathbf 1_{y_i=-x}]$, i.e. $R$ witnesses with error $p\in(0,1/2)$ whose errors are independent conditionally on $x$. $W_{\min}(R,p,\eta)$ is the least $W$ for which some protocol reaches total-variation error $\le\eta$ on both inputs.`,
    statement: r`Let $L=-\tfrac12\log\bigl(4p(1-p)\bigr)>0$. Then for the exact target ($\eta=0$) $\tfrac{R}{2}\,L-O(\log R)\;\le\;\log\bigl(1+W_{\min}(R,p,0)\bigr)\;\le\;R\,L+O(\log R)$. For a fixed tolerance $\eta>0$, $W_{\min}(R,p,\eta)\le\pi/\sqrt{2\eta}+O(1)$ uniformly in $R$: the exponential cost is a cost of exact conditional independence only.`,
    sketch: r`(1) Block structure. $U$ commutes with the total charge and the registers are neutral, so $U$ is block-diagonal over total-charge sectors $m$. For $\omega_A=\lvert c\rangle\langle c\rvert$, $c=\sum_n c_n\lvert n\rangle$, the sector-$m$ component of $\lvert\pm x\rangle\otimes\lvert c\rangle$ is $(c_m\lvert0,m\rangle\pm c_{m-1}\lvert1,m-1\rangle)/\sqrt2$. (2) Information bound. Within a sector the two components are pure with equal norm; the best achievable total-variation distance between the register outputs for $x=\pm$ is $\sum_m\lvert c_mc_{m-1}\rvert\le\cos\!\bigl(\pi/(W+2)\bigr)$, the top eigenvalue bound for the adjacency of a path on $W+1$ vertices; mixed $\omega_A$ cannot do better by convexity. (3) Necessity. Any protocol realising $P^\star$ exactly must realise $d_{\rm TV}(P^\star(\cdot\mid+),P^\star(\cdot\mid-))=\Delta_R(p)$ with $1-\Delta_R=\sum_{\mathbf y}\min\{P^\star(\mathbf y\mid+),P^\star(\mathbf y\mid-)\}\asymp(4p(1-p))^{R/2}$ up to polynomial factors, so $\Delta_R\le\cos(\pi/(W+2))$ forces $W+2\gtrsim\pi/\sqrt{2(1-\Delta_R)}$, i.e. $\log(1+W)\ge\tfrac R2L-O(\log R)$. (4) Sufficiency. Take $c_n$ uniform: every interior sector discriminates $x$ perfectly and the uninformative weight is $q=1/(W+1)$. Write $P^\star=(1-q)\tilde P+qD$ with $D(\mathbf y)\propto\min\{P^\star(\mathbf y\mid+),P^\star(\mathbf y\mid-)\}$; $\tilde P\ge0$ exactly when $q\le1-\Delta_R$, so $W+1\ge(1-\Delta_R)^{-1}$ suffices: $\log(1+W)\le RL+O(\log R)$. (5) Tolerance. With $\tilde P=P^\star$ the output is within $q$ of the target, so $q\le\eta$, hence $W\lesssim\pi/\sqrt{2\eta}$, suffices for every $R$.`,
    open: r`The exact constant: is $\lim_R\log(1+W_{\min}(R,p,0))/R$ equal to $L/2$, to $L$, or strictly between? Step (2) bounds total variation only; whether the sine-profile reference that saturates it can also realise the required pair of output distributions (a Blackwell-order question for pure-state dichotomies) decides the constant. Also open: the crossover of $W_{\min}(R,p,\eta)$ between the polynomial regime $\eta=R^{-\alpha}$ and the exponential regime $\eta=e^{-\alpha R}$, and the variant requiring the marginals to equal $p$ exactly.`,
    baseline: "Wigner–Araki–Yanase theorem and its asymmetry-resource form (Ahmadi–Jennings–Rudolph, NJP 15, 013057 (2013)); size–accuracy trade-offs for quantum reference frames (Tajima–Takagi–Kuramochi, arXiv:2006.14247); covariant catalysis and reference-frame degradation (van Luijk–Werner–Wilming, Quantum 7, 1166 (2023)); quantum Darwinism as motivation (Zurek, Nat. Phys. 5, 181 (2009))." },
];

